1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,828
Graham Tolliver struck back in the first week of
December, and he struck the way frightened

2
00:00:13,848 --> 00:00:19,080
rich men always strike: with lawyers, with
money, and with gossip, all at once.

3
00:00:19,649 --> 00:00:25,866
The lawyers came first. A complaint filed in the
Delaware Court of Chancery, forty-one

4
00:00:25,886 --> 00:00:32,466
pages long, alleging that Doctor Albert Wesker had
induced Verdant Axis to sign the archive

5
00:00:32,486 --> 00:00:39,137
licence through fraudulent oral representations of
exclusivity. It was a weak case. Everyone

6
00:00:39,157 --> 00:00:45,156
who read it knew it was a weak case. It was not
meant to win. It was meant to cost.

7
00:00:45,728 --> 00:00:51,757
The money came second. On the same Tuesday, two
members of Umbrella's staff received job

8
00:00:51,777 --> 00:00:57,668
offers from Verdant Axis. Tomás Reyes was offered a
senior technician's role at nearly

9
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:03,718
twice his salary. Evelyn Marsh was offered a position
as associate director of research,

10
00:01:03,738 --> 00:01:09,973
with her own lab, her own budget, and a signing bonus
larger than everything she had earned

11
00:01:09,993 --> 00:01:12,516
in the previous three years combined.

12
00:01:13,087 --> 00:01:18,588
Evelyn forwarded hers to the Director within four
minutes of receiving it. She added one

13
00:01:18,608 --> 00:01:19,466
word above it.

14
00:01:20,037 --> 00:01:20,754
No.

15
00:01:22,123 --> 00:01:24,135
Tomás did not forward his.

16
00:01:25,704 --> 00:01:31,192
He sat in his car in the parking lot that evening for
forty minutes, looking at the offer,

17
00:01:31,212 --> 00:01:36,394
and at the banking app that told him he still owed
a hundred and six thousand dollars

18
00:01:36,414 --> 00:01:41,596
for a degree in biology he had been told would
change his life. He liked Umbrella. He

19
00:01:41,616 --> 00:01:46,860
liked the Director, in the way you like a
thunderstorm. But a hundred and six thousand

20
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,143
dollars is a very specific number, and it
does not care what you like.

21
00:01:51,713 --> 00:01:57,542
At nine o'clock there was a knock on his car window.
He jumped so hard he hit his head

22
00:01:57,562 --> 00:01:58,359
on the roof.

23
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:04,636
The Director was standing in the rain, beside the
car, holding a folded sheet of paper.

24
00:02:04,656 --> 00:02:10,099
He had no umbrella. He did not seem to be getting
wet in any way that bothered him.

25
00:02:10,788 --> 00:02:12,527
Roll the window down, Tomás.

26
00:02:13,048 --> 00:02:15,233
I — Doc, I was going to tell
you, I swear —

27
00:02:15,752 --> 00:02:16,884
I know. Read this.

28
00:02:17,405 --> 00:02:23,363
It was a letter. It gave Tomás Reyes a new title,
head of digital, which did not yet mean

29
00:02:23,383 --> 00:02:29,342
anything. It gave him a salary exactly one dollar
higher than Verdant's. And it contained

30
00:02:29,362 --> 00:02:35,052
a clause, drafted by Pell, under which Umbrella
would pay off the full balance of his

31
00:02:35,072 --> 00:02:40,830
student loans over three years, provided he was
still employed at the end of each one.

32
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,334
This is — Doc. This is more than —

33
00:02:43,854 --> 00:02:49,501
Verdant is offering you a job. I'm offering you a
job that doesn't exist yet. There's

34
00:02:49,521 --> 00:02:52,167
a difference. One of them has a ceiling.

35
00:02:53,487 --> 00:02:59,114
Tomás signed it on the steering wheel, in the dome
light, with a pen the Director handed

36
00:02:59,134 --> 00:03:01,552
him. The ink ran a little in the rain.

37
00:03:02,122 --> 00:03:07,968
It was, the Director would later calculate, the
cheapest loyalty he ever bought with money.

38
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:11,062
Every other kind he bought with
something worse.

39
00:03:12,832 --> 00:03:18,974
The gossip came third, and it was the cleverest.
Someone at Verdant had noticed the Shenzhen

40
00:03:18,994 --> 00:03:25,135
licence, and by the middle of December, a certain kind
of website had begun to ask a certain

41
00:03:25,155 --> 00:03:31,028
kind of question: who was this Albert Wesker, with no
American history, who had appeared

42
00:03:31,048 --> 00:03:35,916
from nowhere, and sold American biological
samples to a company in China?

43
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:42,133
That is good work. He is making you foreign. It
is the oldest trick there is.

44
00:03:42,653 --> 00:03:43,401
You'd know.

45
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:48,253
I would. And I know how it ends when
you do nothing about it.

46
00:03:48,774 --> 00:03:52,893
I'm not going to do nothing. I'm going to let
someone else do something.

47
00:03:54,913 --> 00:03:58,699
Hannah Price's story ran on the
seventeenth of December.

48
00:03:59,269 --> 00:04:04,858
It was the best piece of journalism the Wilmington
Ledger had published in a decade, and

49
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:10,277
she had checked every line of it three times,
because she knew who had pointed her at

50
00:04:10,297 --> 00:04:14,038
it, and she was determined that
nothing in it would be his.

51
00:04:14,606 --> 00:04:20,974
Nothing in it was. Every fact was her own. The three
trial sites with impossibly low dropout

52
00:04:20,994 --> 00:04:27,084
rates. The Maryland clinic network that ran them. The
limited partnership that owned the

53
00:04:27,104 --> 00:04:33,194
network. And the general partner of the limited
partnership: Curtis Vane, brother-in-law

54
00:04:33,214 --> 00:04:39,234
of Graham Tolliver, who had been paid four point two
million dollars in trial fees over

55
00:04:39,254 --> 00:04:45,205
two years, and who had, according to three former
nurses Hannah had found in two weeks

56
00:04:45,225 --> 00:04:51,176
of phone calls, quietly removed patients from the
study who were not responding to the

57
00:04:51,196 --> 00:04:51,524
drug.

58
00:04:52,093 --> 00:04:58,144
By noon, the story had been picked up by two
national wire services. By one, the Food

59
00:04:58,164 --> 00:05:04,572
and Drug Administration had announced that it was,
quote, reviewing the data. By the close

60
00:05:04,592 --> 00:05:10,143
of trading, Verdant Axis Therapeutics had lost
sixty-one percent of its value.

61
00:05:10,713 --> 00:05:16,679
Graham Tolliver watched it happen from his corner
office, on four screens, with his hand

62
00:05:16,699 --> 00:05:23,276
over his mouth. At four o'clock his general counsel came
in and closed the door. At four-fifteen,

63
00:05:23,296 --> 00:05:27,834
his wife called to ask why her brother was
not answering his phone.

64
00:05:29,403 --> 00:05:35,636
Across the river, in a corner office with a water
stain shaped like Florida on the ceiling,

65
00:05:35,656 --> 00:05:41,615
Albert Wesker watched the same line fall on a
nine-year-old monitor, and did not smile.

66
00:05:42,185 --> 00:05:45,730
Smiling was for amateurs. He was
doing arithmetic.

67
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:53,659
The arithmetic was simple, and it was impossible. At
the close on the seventeenth, the

68
00:05:53,679 --> 00:06:00,083
whole of Verdant Axis — its glass building, its hundred
and forty employees, its laboratories,

69
00:06:00,103 --> 00:06:06,235
its drug pipeline, and ninety million dollars in cash
— could be bought on the open market

70
00:06:06,255 --> 00:06:09,515
for about a hundred and seventy
million dollars.

71
00:06:10,086 --> 00:06:11,743
Umbrella had two million.

72
00:06:12,312 --> 00:06:17,600
He was still considering the problem at ten o'clock
that night when his phone rang, from

73
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,892
a number in Manhattan he did not know.

74
00:06:20,581 --> 00:06:25,838
Doctor Wesker. My name is Catherine Adeyemi. I'm a
partner at Keel Harbour Capital. We

75
00:06:25,858 --> 00:06:31,237
specialise in distressed life sciences. I won't keep
you long. I only have one question.

76
00:06:31,757 --> 00:06:32,473
Go on.

77
00:06:32,992 --> 00:06:34,829
You wrote that article, didn't you?

78
00:06:36,548 --> 00:06:42,160
He did not answer immediately. It was the first time
in three months that a question had

79
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,079
arrived before he expected it.

80
00:06:44,769 --> 00:06:47,545
Hannah Price wrote that article.
It's very good.

81
00:06:48,064 --> 00:06:52,961
Hannah Price wrote it. You aimed it. Your company signs
a licence with Verdant in September.

82
00:06:52,981 --> 00:06:57,612
Verdant tries to take your building in October.
Tolliver's lawyers sue you in December.

83
00:06:57,632 --> 00:07:02,155
And three weeks later, a local reporter nobody's
heard of finds a four-million-dollar

84
00:07:02,175 --> 00:07:06,752
family scheme buried in a clinic network in
Maryland. I've been doing this for fifteen

85
00:07:06,772 --> 00:07:11,296
years, Doctor Wesker. Nothing in this business
happens three weeks later by accident.

86
00:07:11,817 --> 00:07:12,777
And if I did?

87
00:07:13,295 --> 00:07:17,727
Then I'd like to buy you a very expensive dinner and
discuss how you intend to pay for

88
00:07:17,747 --> 00:07:19,952
the rest of the company you've
just broken.

89
00:07:21,973 --> 00:07:26,762
She came to Wilmington herself, on the Monday,
on the eight o'clock train.

90
00:07:27,331 --> 00:07:32,914
Catherine Adeyemi was thirty-eight, born in Lagos,
raised in South London, educated at

91
00:07:32,934 --> 00:07:38,516
Cambridge and on the trading floors of the City, and
she had left a very senior job at

92
00:07:38,536 --> 00:07:44,444
a very old bank after she had reported one of its
partners to the regulator, and discovered

93
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:50,176
what happens to people who do that. She had been at
Keel Harbour for four years. She was

94
00:07:50,196 --> 00:07:55,322
very good at it. She was also, though she did
not tell people this, very bored.

95
00:07:55,892 --> 00:08:01,601
She looked around the lobby, and at the water stain,
and at the nine-year-old monitors,

96
00:08:01,621 --> 00:08:05,485
and at the man in black who rose to
meet her, and she said:

97
00:08:06,175 --> 00:08:10,007
Well. This is either the worst investment I've
ever seen, or the best one.

98
00:08:10,528 --> 00:08:11,511
Which do you think?

99
00:08:12,031 --> 00:08:13,739
I think I'll know after lunch.

100
00:08:15,259 --> 00:08:19,911
They talked for three hours. It was not the
kind of meeting he was used to.

101
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:25,823
The Command Presence did not work on her — or not
properly. He felt it reach for her,

102
00:08:25,843 --> 00:08:31,437
the way a hand reaches for a railing in the dark, and
he felt it slide off. The Cold-Read

103
00:08:31,457 --> 00:08:37,178
worked, but what it showed him was a woman reading him
back with nearly the same precision:

104
00:08:37,198 --> 00:08:42,666
amusement, calculation, and something else,
flickering in and out, that took him a long

105
00:08:42,686 --> 00:08:46,956
time to identify, because he had so rarely
seen it aimed at himself.

106
00:08:47,524 --> 00:08:53,143
It was respect. And, beneath it, a small bright
thread of alarm, that she was choosing

107
00:08:53,163 --> 00:08:55,765
— consciously, deliberately — to ignore.

108
00:08:57,256 --> 00:09:02,384
Here's the structure. Keel Harbour lends Umbrella sixty
million on a bridge, secured against

109
00:09:02,404 --> 00:09:07,420
your archive licences and the building, at a rate I'll
describe to my partners as punitive

110
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:12,400
and you'll describe to your staff as generous. You
buy just under five percent of Verdant

111
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:17,380
on the open market this week, quietly, below the
reporting line. Then you cross it, file,

112
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:22,192
and in January you launch a tender for control at a
premium to a price that's still on

113
00:09:22,212 --> 00:09:26,948
the floor. The board will be desperate. Half of
them will be facing shareholder suits

114
00:09:26,968 --> 00:09:30,473
over the trial data. You offer them a
clean exit. They take it.

115
00:09:30,994 --> 00:09:31,849
And Tolliver?

116
00:09:32,369 --> 00:09:37,923
Tolliver is a man whose brother-in-law is about to be
indicted. Tolliver is not your problem.

117
00:09:37,943 --> 00:09:39,601
Tolliver is his own problem.

118
00:09:40,922 --> 00:09:41,969
And what do you want?

119
00:09:42,489 --> 00:09:47,519
Three things. If it closes, I want to be your chief
financial officer. I want equity.

120
00:09:47,539 --> 00:09:51,322
And I want to know, at all times, where
the money actually goes.

121
00:09:52,841 --> 00:09:54,796
He looked at her for a long moment.

122
00:09:55,365 --> 00:10:01,052
The Adolf half of him did not like her at all. She
was clever, and she was not afraid,

123
00:10:01,072 --> 00:10:06,825
and the old voice noticed — with the reflexes of the
poison it had once preached — that

124
00:10:06,845 --> 00:10:12,930
she was not the kind of person it had ever allowed at
its table. Wesker noticed it noticing,

125
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:18,836
and found it contemptible. Not as a matter of
morality, which interested him very little.

126
00:10:18,856 --> 00:10:24,476
As a matter of intelligence. And the Wesker half
understood what he was being offered

127
00:10:24,496 --> 00:10:30,316
— a first-rate financial mind who could not be
summoned, could not be ordered, and could

128
00:10:30,336 --> 00:10:34,696
therefore do the one thing none of his
loyal purchases ever could.

129
00:10:35,264 --> 00:10:37,356
She could tell him when he was wrong.

130
00:10:38,048 --> 00:10:38,620
Done.

131
00:10:39,139 --> 00:10:40,346
Just like that?

132
00:10:40,866 --> 00:10:44,644
I don't negotiate with people I've
already decided to hire.

133
00:10:46,163 --> 00:10:52,056
She laughed — a short, surprised laugh — and shook
his hand. His grip was very cool. She

134
00:10:52,076 --> 00:10:58,104
noticed that, too, and filed it with the other things
she was deciding not to think about.

135
00:10:58,673 --> 00:11:02,981
He had agreed to all three of her conditions.
He would honour two of them.

136
00:11:05,052 --> 00:11:10,980
Graham Tolliver's last move was the stupidest one, and
he made it the night after Catherine

137
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,025
Adeyemi's visit.

138
00:11:12,596 --> 00:11:18,394
He had heard, from Dale Kuchar, a rambling story
about a machine in a basement that had

139
00:11:18,414 --> 00:11:24,145
not been there before. He had decided — in the way
that desperate men decide things at

140
00:11:24,165 --> 00:11:30,096
two in the morning — that if he could prove Umbrella
was running an unlicensed biological

141
00:11:30,116 --> 00:11:35,981
facility, he could take the whole thing to the state
and bury it. So he paid two retired

142
00:11:36,001 --> 00:11:41,866
police officers who did private work, and they went
to the building, and they forced the

143
00:11:41,886 --> 00:11:46,013
basement fire door with a pry bar at
two-forty in the morning.

144
00:11:46,581 --> 00:11:51,951
They had flashlights and a camera. They found the
freezers. They found the grey unit in

145
00:11:51,971 --> 00:11:53,314
the corner, breathing.

146
00:11:53,884 --> 00:11:55,947
One of them lifted the camera.

147
00:11:57,517 --> 00:11:59,379
Hale was standing in front of him.

148
00:11:59,947 --> 00:12:05,632
Neither man had heard him arrive. Neither man had
heard Gus, either, who was standing

149
00:12:05,652 --> 00:12:11,604
two feet to Hale's left, holding a mop, with an
expression of mild professional interest.

150
00:12:11,624 --> 00:12:13,214
Neither of them blinked.

151
00:12:13,904 --> 00:12:15,836
Good morning, gentlemen.

152
00:12:17,556 --> 00:12:23,250
What happened in the next four minutes, the two men
never told anyone. Nobody was hurt.

153
00:12:23,270 --> 00:12:28,964
The Director had been very clear about that, and
Hale did not do things he had not been

154
00:12:28,984 --> 00:12:34,679
told to do. There was no violence. There were only
four minutes in a dark basement with

155
00:12:34,699 --> 00:12:40,393
two men who did not blink, and a machine that
breathed, and — one of the men would say,

156
00:12:40,413 --> 00:12:46,108
years later, drunk, to a bartender in Arizona who
did not believe him — a small frosted

157
00:12:46,128 --> 00:12:52,020
window in the side of the machine, through which, for
a moment, something had looked back.

158
00:12:53,590 --> 00:12:59,444
They left without the camera. Both of them resigned
from private work the next morning.

159
00:12:59,464 --> 00:13:02,686
One moved to Arizona. The other
joined a church.

160
00:13:03,255 --> 00:13:06,542
Neither of them ever called Graham
Tolliver again.

161
00:13:09,363 --> 00:13:13,098
Assessment. Five thousand six
hundred Points.

162
00:13:19,788 --> 00:13:25,928
On Christmas Eve, Umbrella filed with the Securities
and Exchange Commission that it had

163
00:13:25,948 --> 00:13:32,299
acquired five point two percent of Verdant Axis
Therapeutics, and intended to seek control.

164
00:13:32,869 --> 00:13:38,635
On Christmas Day, Graham Tolliver read the filing at
his dining table, in front of his

165
00:13:38,655 --> 00:13:44,421
wife, and his children, and his brother-in-law's
empty chair, and understood, at last,

166
00:13:44,441 --> 00:13:46,102
who he had been fighting.
